    pr_ref:  # 83db
    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 5).**\n\n**Symptom.** Running `hypergumbo run . --out /tmp/hg-gzip.json --gzip` writes **gzipped binary content** to a file named `.json`:\n\n```bash\n$ file /tmp/hg-gzip.json\n/tmp/hg-gzip.json: gzip compressed data\n$ python3 -c 'import json; json.load(open(\"/tmp/hg-gzip.json\"))'\nUnicodeDecodeError: 'utf-8' codec can't decode byte 0x8b in position 1: invalid start byte\n```\n\nNo warning, no rename, no refusal. The user (or downstream consumer) ends up with a file whose extension lies about its content type.\n\n**Documented pairing.** `CHANGELOG.md` line 140 says explicitly:\n\n> \"`--gzip` writes main + budget-tier outputs as gzipped JSON with byte-identical content under `gunzip`; **pairs with `--out foo.json.gz`**\"\n\nSo the intended invocation is `--out foo.json.gz --gzip` (or equivalent). But the CLI accepts the mismatched pairing silently. The CLI gives no signal at runtime that the user is producing a misnamed file.\n\n**Why it matters.**\n\n- Standard JSON tooling (`jq`, `cat`, `python -c 'import json'`, IDE JSON viewers) all fail on the binary-named-`.json` file.\n- Build pipelines that pattern-match by extension will pass the file to JSON readers and fail downstream.\n- The file is not self-identifying — `file foo.json` reports \"gzip compressed data\" but a user listing the directory has no way to tell.\n\n**Fix proposal.**\n\nThree viable approaches, easiest first:\n\n1. **Auto-append `.gz`.** If `--gzip` is set and `--out` doesn't end in `.gz`, automatically append `.gz` to the output path. Print a one-line info note explaining the rename. Smallest user-facing surprise.\n\n2. **Refuse the mismatch.** If `--gzip` is set and `--out` doesn't end in `.gz`, error with a clear message: \"Use --out <path>.json.gz when --gzip is set.\" Forces the user to be explicit. Friendlier than silent miswriting.\n\n3. **Warn-only.** Emit a stderr warning when the extensions mismatch, but proceed. Users who know what they're doing aren't blocked; everyone else gets a clue.\n\nRecommended: (1) for the lowest-friction UX, with a CI gate that errors if a hypergumbo `run --gzip --out *.json` produces a file the JSON parser can't open. (1) makes the failure mode disappear at the source.\n\n**Severity.** Low — there is a documented workaround (`--out foo.json.gz`), the fix is small (1-line check at the CLI argv handler), and no data is lost (the gzipped content is valid). But it adds friction the tool can prevent.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/cli.py:5624-5631` — `--no-sketch-fan-out` and `--gzip` arg setup\n- `CHANGELOG.md` line 138-140 — documents the pairing intent\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/safety_zones.py::user_out_open_json_dump_gzip` — gzip-writer helper\n\n**Notebook reference.** `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/notebookjournal_05162026_0438.md` Round 5 section."  # 83db
    fields: {}  # 83db
